1
00:05:37,500 --> 00:05:43,500
The first motion picture in CinemaScope--the modern
miracle you see without glasses!

2
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:21,000
The Robe is a drama, history film directed by Henry Koster.

3
00:16:52,500 --> 00:16:58,500
Drunk and disillusioned Roman, Marcellus Gallio, wins Jesus'
robe in a dice game after the crucifixion.

4
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:36,000
Marcellus has never been a man of faith like his slave, Demetrius, but when Demetrius escapes with
the robe, Marcellus experiences disturbing visions and feels guilty for his actions.

5
00:28:07,500 --> 00:28:13,500
Convinced that destroying the robe will cure him, Marcellus sets out to find
Demetrius — and discovers his Christian faith along the way.

6
00:33:45,000 --> 00:33:51,000
Marcellus Gallio, played by Richard Burton.

7
00:39:22,500 --> 00:39:28,500
Diana, played by Jean Simmons.

8
00:45:00,000 --> 00:45:06,000
Demetrius, played by Victor Mature.

9
00:50:37,500 --> 00:50:43,500
Pontius Pilate, played by Richard Boone.

10
00:56:15,000 --> 00:56:21,000
Abidor, played by Leon Askin.

11
01:01:52,500 --> 01:01:58,500
Peter, played by Michael Rennie.

12
01:07:30,000 --> 01:07:36,000
The Robe is a 1953 American  Biblical epic film that tells the story of a Roman military
tribune who commands the unit that is responsible for the crucifixion of Jesus.

13
01:13:07,500 --> 01:13:13,500
The film was released by 20th Century Fox and was the first film
released in the widescreen process CinemaScope.

14
01:18:45,000 --> 01:18:51,000
Like other early CinemaScope films, The Robe was shot with Henri
Chrétien's original Hypergonar anamorphic lenses.

15
01:24:22,500 --> 01:24:28,500
The film was directed by Henry Koster and produced by Frank Ross.

16
01:30:00,000 --> 01:30:06,000
The screenplay was adapted from Lloyd C.

17
01:35:37,500 --> 01:35:43,500
Douglas's 1942 novel by Gina Kaus, Albert Maltz, and Philip Dunne—although Maltz's place among the
blacklisted Hollywood 10 led to his being denied his writing credit for many years.

18
01:41:15,000 --> 01:41:21,000
The score was composed by Alfred Newman, and the
cinematography was by Leon Shamroy.

19
01:46:52,500 --> 01:46:58,500
The film stars Richard Burton, Jean Simmons, Victor Mature and Michael Rennie, and
co-stars Jay Robinson, Dean Jagger, Torin Thatcher, Richard Boone, Betta St.

20
01:52:30,000 --> 01:52:36,000
John, Jeff Morrow, Ernest Thesiger, and others.

21
01:58:07,500 --> 01:58:13,500
A sequel, Demetrius and the Gladiators (1954),
continues from where The Robe ends.

22
02:03:45,000 --> 02:03:51,000
The film explores themes of palestine, roman empire,
christianity, crucifixion, bible.

23
02:09:22,500 --> 02:09:28,500
The tone is dark and emotional.
